Since 1 July 2026, flexi-jobs are no longer reserved for the horeca and retail sectors. This form of supplementary employment, highly advantageous from a tax perspective, is now open to virtually all sectors in Belgium. For your SME, it's a new flexible recruitment option. Here's what it changes in practice.

## Flexi-jobs: the principle in a nutshell

A flexi-job is a supplementary employment contract that allows a worker to top up their income by working for another employer. The principle: virtually no social security contributions for the employer, virtually no tax for the worker (up to €18,000 per year since 2025). In return: no social rights for these hours.

Until 30 June 2026, only a few sectors were eligible: horeca, retail, bakeries, events. Since 1 July, it's a general opening. Your IT SME, your consultancy firm, your marketing agency, your production workshop can in principle take on flexi-job workers.

## The conditions for hiring on a flexi-job basis

Your SME can hire a flexi-job worker under two conditions:

**1. The worker must already be employed elsewhere**

They must work at least 4/5 time with another employer, or be retired. The idea: it's a supplement, not a main income.

**2. You cannot hire your own employee on a flexi-job basis**

You cannot convert the hours of an existing employee into a flexi-job in the same quarter. Exception: temporary workers can do flexi-jobs elsewhere, as long as they are not sent to the same client.

Another limit: you cannot hire someone on a flexi-job basis who already works in a related company (unless they are full-time elsewhere). This aims to prevent abuse between subsidiaries.

## Sectoral opt-out: check first

All sectors are theoretically eligible, unless your joint committee has decided on an "opt-out". The social partners in your sector can exclude flexi-jobs, temporarily or partially. Some care sectors have limited the volume of flexi-jobs to 20% of total staff, for example.

Before you start, consult your social secretariat or your accountancy firm to verify that your joint committee hasn't excluded flexi-jobs. Sectoral decisions evolve: a sector may initially refuse, then authorise later.

## Salary and declarations: the practical guide

The minimum flexi-job wage is €12.78 per hour (holiday pay included, March 2026). For horeca, a specific ceiling of €21 applies. For other sectors, there is no ceiling: you can pay more if the profile justifies it.

On the administrative side, you must:

- Sign a framework agreement before the first assignment
- Draw up a flexi-job employment contract
- Submit a Dimona FLX before each assignment
- Record the hours worked
- Pay a social security contribution of 25% on the gross salary (no standard ONSS contributions)

The worker receives their salary virtually net: no withholding tax deducted (as long as they remain below the annual ceiling of €18,000).

## Why it's attractive for an SME

If your activity experiences temporary peaks — accounting closure period, product launch, project overload — flexi-jobs allow you to strengthen your teams without increasing your structural payroll costs.

Concrete example: you're a consultant, you land a large project over three months. Rather than hiring a junior on a fixed-term contract with all the charges, you take on a flexi-worker already employed elsewhere, who's looking for supplementary income. You pay 25% contributions, not 40%.

But be careful: flexi-jobs remain supplementary. You won't build a stable team with this status. And you must accept that these workers have no social protection through these hours.

